sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch02-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Some simple cellular automata, such as rule 110, are more complex than random-looking ones like rule 30: they mix regular regions with interacting localized structures, so that even average properties of their behavior become very difficult to predict.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Some cellular automata make even average properties hard to predict";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 2, “The Crucial Experiment”; section “How Do Simple Programs Behave?” (from p. 23), p. 31. Scope: elementary cellular automata with localized structures, especially rule 110"
}
